# ML Pipeline Threat Models

This directory contains threat models for the scoped portion of the ML pipeline:

1. Data moving from external and internal sources into the landing zone.
2. Data ingestion from the landing zone into the ingestion layer.

Out of scope for these models:

- Feature engineering
- Model training
- Model registry
- Model serving
- Monitoring and inference feedback loops
- Business application consumers

## Threat Model Inventory

| Stage | Threat model | Scope |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Source to landing zone | [01-source-to-landing-zone.md](01-source-to-landing-zone.md) | Files, events, API payloads, database extracts, and third-party feeds arriving at the landing zone. |
| Data ingestion layer | [02-data-ingestion-layer.md](02-data-ingestion-layer.md) | Validation, parsing, normalization, metadata capture, quarantine, and controlled movement from landing into ingestion storage. |

## Shared Security Objectives

- Preserve data integrity before it is trusted by downstream ML workflows.
- Prevent unauthorized data submission, modification, or deletion.
- Detect malicious, malformed, stale, poisoned, or misrouted data early.
- Maintain source identity, lineage, and ingestion auditability.
- Restrict sensitive data exposure during transfer, landing, validation, and parsing.
- Isolate untrusted data until validation and policy checks complete.

## Shared Assumptions

- Source systems can be internal applications, partner systems, vendor feeds, object storage drops, message streams, APIs, or database exports.
- The landing zone is treated as an untrusted or semi-trusted boundary because data has not yet been validated.
- All data accepted into the pipeline should have source identity, schema/version metadata, arrival timestamp, and integrity metadata where possible.
- Downstream ML pipeline stages must not consume raw landing data directly unless explicitly approved by policy.
